Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "An Introduction to ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] 1996-98 Klaus Sutner \n\[Copyright] 2000 Dana S. \
Scott\n",
    FontSize->14],
  StyleBox["Version of 28 August 2000",
    FontSize->14]
}], "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Select and doubleclick on the first right-hand bracket below to begin \
reading."
}], "Text",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, Inherited}, {Inherited, 20}},
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Frontend and Notebooks"
}], "Section",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " really consists of two independent parts: the ",
  StyleBox["kernel",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", where all the computations are performed, and the ",
  StyleBox["frontend",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", which is used to send input to the kernel and then display the output \
generated by the kernel. In some operating systems, you can use a primitive \
command-line interface to communicate directly with the kernel, but we will \
usually deal with the ",
  StyleBox["notebook",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["frontend",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Notebooks are the most important feature of using the ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " frontend.  A ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " notebook is an interactively editable ",
  StyleBox["electronic document",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that may contain ",
  StyleBox["text",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["input",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["output",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["graphics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Notebooks are ",
  StyleBox["hierarchically organized",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "\[LongDash]not linearly, as in an ordinary text.  This style of \
organization is of great help in editing and using notebooks."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Doubleclick on the next right-hand bracket below to continue reading."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["About Cells", "Subsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The fundamental unit in a notebook is a ",
  StyleBox["cell",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  These cells are indicated along the right edge of the notebook by \
various brackets.  Clicking and double-clicking on these brackets allows one \
to manipulate the cells in various ways and permits ",
  StyleBox["deleting",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["copying",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of whole cells. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In particular, to ",
  "execute",
  " a ",
  StyleBox["command",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " contained in a cell, ",
  StyleBox["select",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the cell by clicking the mouse on the bracket (or, for that matter, \
anywhere in the cell).  Then press the ",
  StyleBox["Enter",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " key\[LongDash]not the ",
  StyleBox["Return",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " key!!!, as that produces only a linefeed.  Alternatively, you can use a \
",
  StyleBox["Shift-Return",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ". \nTry to execute the next cell:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["123 + 456", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that when you open a notebook, you are in frontend reading/editing \
mode, ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in compute mode.  The first time you ask to execute a command cell, the \
systems pauses to start up the kernel and then gives the asked-for output \
(with numbers for input and output indicated on the left).  The next time you \
ask for computational output, this pause will not be necessary, as the kernel \
is already running."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For other commands and ways of entering data and information into the \
Notebook, experiment with the pull-down menus, so you get an idea what \
operations are available, and where you can look for them.  There are \
key-stroke abbreviations for all the important operations, but don't worry \
about these yet.  Look in particular under the ",
  StyleBox["Edit",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["Cell",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["Style",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " menus. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that some cells do not contain executable commands, such as this one. \
  If you try to execute them, nothing happens\[LongDash]except for a ",
  StyleBox["beep",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Go ahead, try this one!).  If you go to the ",
  StyleBox["Help",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " menu and open ",
  StyleBox["Why the Beep?",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ", you will get an explanatory dialog box.  (The beep can sound in other \
situations, as well.)"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You can tell what kind of cell the cursor is currently in by selecting the \
",
  StyleBox["Cell",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " option at the top of the screen and looking through the submenus.  If it \
is marked ",
  StyleBox["inactive",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ",  the cell cannot be executed.  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
If you see a little triangular flag at the lower right hand side of \
a cell, you can double click on the bracket to open another cell in the \
hierarchical organization. You have already been doing this to read this far. \
 Test the feature out further by navigating through cells in the following \
sample part of a notebook. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A Sample Section in a Notebook", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Cells within cells", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Text in a cell. 
Followed by an input cell. Execute it, and the corresponding output cell will \
appear. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Range[10]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["And more text, followed by another input cell. ", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["RotateLeft[ Range[10], 3 ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" ... cells within cells", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["A little more text here. ", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" and more cells", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["and more text...", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" and more cells ...", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["more text ...", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Closing cells", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Select the bracket at the right for this sample section.  Type ",
  StyleBox["Command-Shift-}",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ", and the big cell and all its subcells will ",
  StyleBox["close",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Command-Shift-{",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " is the ",
  StyleBox["open-all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " command for a cell and all of its subcells. A simple double-click on a \
cell bracket closes only the ",
  StyleBox["top level",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the hierarchy."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
  "Some ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Examples"
}], "Section",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The examples below use many commands in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that you are not yet familiar with.  Don't worry about any of the details \
yet, as this is just to give a first, vague idea of the ",
  StyleBox["scope",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "However, you should familiarize yourself with one particular feature as \
soon as possible, since you will use\[LongDash]and perhaps curse\[LongDash]it \
often: ",
  StyleBox["the built-in help mechanism",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ",called the ",
  StyleBox["Help Browser",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ".  You can find more information about what ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " does in Wolfram's reference manual (a mere 1500 pages for ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " 4.0 in the printed version), and in any number of other books about ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", but it is almost always more convenient to use the on-line help instead. \
 The browser also puts the ",
  StyleBox["whole",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " book on line.  Few software systems have as extensive on-line \
documentation."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  " It will be a good idea to go through this notebook more than once and at \
your own speed, getting help for all the commands used and experimenting on \
your own, until you become comfortable with notebooks and some of the most \
basic commands. Do not worry about ",
  StyleBox["programming",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " just yet. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Getting going", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As mentioned, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " has a number of ",
  StyleBox["on-line help facilities",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". In conjunction with a good amount of experimentation these facilities \
should be enough to make you understand the workings of a command.  Here is \
some initial advice."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Getting started", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["Help",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " menu has the ",
  StyleBox["Help Browser...",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " option to choose.  When the dialog box comes up, there is a ",
  StyleBox["Getting Started/Demos",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " button to click.  As you are learning ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", return to this on-line facility often to check out how the system works. \
 In this present notebook we are giving a less-detailed review of features \
together with an opportunity to learn how to use notebooks."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Information[symbol]", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " keeps track of all the ",
  StyleBox["information",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " associated with a symbol (which may be user-defined or built-in). By \
typing ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(?"symbol"\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(?? "symbol"\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  you can access that information.  You can also type  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Information[symbol]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Execute now the following ?-commands."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?Pi\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["?\[Pi]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["??\[Pi]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Information[\[Pi]]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The last is the ",
  StyleBox["long form",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The ??-command usually gives the extra information of the long form."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(?Plus\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(?? Plus\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In case no special information is being remembered, the symbol is just \
classed as ",
  StyleBox["global",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Clear[f]
?f\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
If a user's definition has been entered (at anytime in the past not \
deleted by a Clear), the ?-command will find it.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[f]\), "\n", 
    \(f[n_] := n\^2 + 5\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?f\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You may use ",
  StyleBox["wildcards",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if you cannot exactly remember the name of what you are looking for. This \
turns out to be extremely helpful.  The first result is just the list of \
possibilities.  You still have to pick the one you want to see its associated \
information. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["?Par*", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?Partition\)\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Calling up the Help Browser", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Place your cursor after the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["i",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["Pi",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " below, and press ",
  StyleBox["Meta-F",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " (in all likelihood using the ",
  StyleBox["Alt-key",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " on your keyboard or the  ",
  StyleBox["Shift-Command-F",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " on a Macintosh). A help browser will then pop up that provides \
information about the selected symbol. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Pi\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The main advantage of the browser over the simple ??-mechanism is that it \
provides more ",
  StyleBox["context",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ": if you look up, say, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["RotateLeft",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", you will find nearby a lot of other similar commands.  This is the way \
to go!!! Try it now."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(RotateLeft\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Some conventions", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "'s built-in commands always start with a ",
  StyleBox["capital letter",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and use internal caps for readability.  For example:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
AppendTo
RotateLeft\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Consequently, it is sound advice for your privately defined functions \
always to start with a ",
  StyleBox["lower-case letter",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", but you may still use internal caps. It is much easier to sort things \
out this way.  (Some authors may not choose to follow this suggestion.)"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Another suggestion: always use ",
  StyleBox["long, descriptive names",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The reason is that when you read some code written some days ago, the \
descriptive names will help you remember what it is for."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In general, just as in ordinary programming, a little extra ",
  StyleBox["commentary",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can make life much more pleasant. It is possible to write incredibly \
cryptic code in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", so comments can be quite crucial.  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Completion", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To facilitate typing of long, descriptive commands, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " has a command-name-completion mechanism. Place your cursor after the \
prefix in the cell below, and press ",
  StyleBox["Meta-k",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["Command-k",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ". A little menu will pop up that gives you several choices for ",
  StyleBox["completion",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Choose one and execute the command"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["??Append", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Completion also works with user-defined objects. ", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["foofoofoo = 5;", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["foofo", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Editing and word processing", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " frontend is a very sophisticated word processor and \
documentation-preparation engine.  A large variety of mathematical symbols \
and formats is available. It takes some time to learn how to use all the \
features.  For a comprehensive survey, go to the ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Book in the ",
  StyleBox["Help Browser",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ", and enter 1.10 in the ",
  StyleBox["Go-To",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " window.  There are several sections there explaining where various kinds \
of symbols and formats come from.  Section 1.10.11 in particular discusses ",
  StyleBox["displaying",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["printing",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Normally, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " displays expressions in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(StandardForm\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " notation.  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(StandardForm\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " differs from traditional mathematical notation in a number of respects, \
but it has the advantage of always being unambiguous.  For example, consider \
the traditional expression ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`f\ \((g + h)\)\)]],
  ".  Is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`f\)]],
  " a variable that is being multiplied by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`g + h\)]],
  ", or is it a function that is being applied to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`g + h\)]],
  "?  In ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(StandardForm\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", these two cases are distinguished from each other by the expressions ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(f\ \((g + h)\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(f[g + h]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", respectively.  Nevertheless, you can convert any expression to \
traditional mathematical notation, regardless of its ambiguities, by \
highlighting the expression and typing ",
  StyleBox["Meta-T",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Basic operations", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Numerics", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 1: Simple calculations", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can be used like an ",
  StyleBox["ordinary calculator",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(2 + 10\), "\n", 
    \(2\ 10\), "\n", 
    \(2\^10\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["All the usual functions are available:", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\@1.8\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Sin[3.1]\), "\n", 
    \(Cos[0]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["And a few not so usual ones:", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Binomial[10, 5]\), "\n", 
    \(PartitionsP[20]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Did you look these functions up in the ",
  StyleBox["Help Browser",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  "?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["WARNING. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "In ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", as you may have noticed, the ",
  StyleBox["square brackets",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " [ and ] are used for ",
  StyleBox["function application",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", not the parentheses ( and ) as is customary in mathematics and computer \
science.  Parentheses are employed just for ",
  StyleBox["grouping,",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\((x + y)\)\ \((x - y)\) + Sin[z]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  But you can output traditional notation, as explained in the section on \
Editing."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 2: Precision", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There are several significant distinctions between ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and a calculator to keep in mind, though: the calculations can be carried \
out with ",
  StyleBox["arbitrary precision",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " understands ",
  StyleBox["symbolic values",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". In fact, it usually tries to maintain the evaluation of symbolic \
expressions in symbolic form, and we have to use the function  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(N[ ... ]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  to get ",
  StyleBox["numerical values",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is a typical example.  Square root of 2  by itself remains ",
  StyleBox["unevaluated",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\@2\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The reason is that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\@2\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a very common quantity which figures in many formulae where the \
decimal approximation is not relevant or helpful for understanding."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But, if we want, we can find the numerical value with ",
  StyleBox["standard precision",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[\@2]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "However, if you ask for the square root of a ",
  StyleBox["floating-point number",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", you get the decimal answer:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\@2.0\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, if we want ",
  StyleBox["higher precision",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " we can ask for it:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[\@2, 50]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And here we get ",
  StyleBox["ridiculous precision",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[\@2, 1000]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 3: Performing more calculations", "Example"],

Cell["Try some more calculations with many digits:", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2\^5000\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(1000!\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\[ExponentialE]\), "\n", 
    \(N[\[ExponentialE], 100]\), "\n", 
    \(\[Pi]\), "\n", 
    \(N[\[Pi], 100]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[\[Pi]\^5]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(pisot = \((2\/\(27 + 3\ \@69\))\)\^\(1/3\) + 
        1\/3\ \((1\/2\ \((27 + 3\ \@69)\))\)\^\(1/3\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[pisot\^30000, 6000]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 4: \[Sum] and \[Product]", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Summations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["products",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are also easily available"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\[Sum]\+\(i = 1\)\%100 i\), "\n", 
    \(\[Sum]\+\(i = 1\)\%100 i\^2\), "\n", 
    \(\[Sum]\+\(i = 1\)\%100 i\^3\), "\n", 
    \(N[\[Sum]\+\(i = 1\)\%100 i\^\(\@2\)]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\[Product]\+\(i = 1\)\%100 i\), "\n", 
    \(\(100!\)\), "\n", 
    \(% == %%\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that in the last line, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(%\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " refers to the ",
  StyleBox["immediately previous",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " output, and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["%%",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to the ",
  StyleBox["second-to-the-last",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " output. Comparing those numbers shows they are equal. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 5: Equality", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Take care in using equality.",
  FontWeight->"Plain"]], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["WARNING. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "In ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", a ",
  StyleBox["test for equality",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " uses the notation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \( == \)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  \nWhereas, a single ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["=",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " indicates ",
  StyleBox["assignment",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", just as in C and C++. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["For example:", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(n = 10\), "\n", 
    \(x1 = \[Sum]\+\(i = 1\)\%n i\), "\n", 
    \(x2 = 1\/2\ n\ \((n + 1)\)\), "\n", 
    \(x1 == x2\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 6: Numerical solutions", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Another task involves equations.  One can solve equations ",
  StyleBox["numerically",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " using ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(NSolve\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Note that the next input assigns an ",
  StyleBox["equation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the variable ",
  StyleBox["eqn",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". The solution is assigned to ",
  StyleBox["sol",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". Got that?"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(eqn = x\^7 - 8\ x\^3 + 12.5\ x - 10.2 == 0\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = NSolve[eqn, x]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The format of the solution is a little strange, we get back a complicated \
list of ",
  StyleBox["substitution rules",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". Using these as rules is very important in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", but for the time being they may be somewhat of a pain.  Suffice it to \
say that we can get the actual values for x by a ",
  StyleBox["substitution",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as follows."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x /. \[InvisibleSpace]sol\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "All the solutions except the last are ",
  StyleBox["complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (where, in the output above, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\[ImaginaryI]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " stands for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\@\(-1\)\)]],
  "). "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 7: Numerical integrals", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Numerical integration",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is also built in:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(NIntegrate[Sin[x]\^\(\[ExponentialE]\^x\), {x, 0, \[Pi]}]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Just how ",
  StyleBox["accurate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " this answer is a different question!"
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Symbolics", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 1: Symbolic solutions", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For many purposes, the symbolic capabilities of ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are often more important than numerical number crunching.  For example, \
we can ",
  StyleBox["solve a quadratic equation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with symbolic coefficients to get a symbolic (algebraic) answer."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(eqn = a\ x\^2 + b\ x + c == 0\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[eqn, x]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note how the answer comes back in the form of ",
  StyleBox["substitution rules",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". As we pointed out before, if you want a ",
  StyleBox["list",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of roots, you can write:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x /. Solve[eqn, x]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 2: Solving a cubic equation", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is a ",
  StyleBox["third degree equation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[x\^3 + a\ x + b == 0, x]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 3: Solving a quartic equation", "Example"],

Cell["\<\
We can also solve more complicated equations, though you may not \
like the output very much!\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[a\ x\^4 + b\ x\^3 + c\ x\^2 + d\ x\^2 + e == 0, x]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 4: Expanding", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Many algebraic manipulations in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are in general are quite easy."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[\((a + b)\)\^10]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["A WARNING.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "In ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", there are often several ways to express the same command.  Which you \
choose all depends on which formula is most convenient. For example, we could \
also have written \n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\((a + b)\)\^10\  // \ Expand\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  \nThe ",
  StyleBox["//",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " notation is similar to Unix pipes and often comes in handy when typing \
long calculations. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  TextAlignment->-0.5,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 5: Factoring", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can ",
  StyleBox["factor",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " algebraic expressions and, needless to say, integers. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\((a + b + c)\)\^10 // Expand\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(% // Factor\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(30!\) - \(12!\)\), "\n", 
    \(FactorInteger[%]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 6: %", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["We can make ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["quick references",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" to previous results.  For instance, the percent sign in the last \
expression of Example 5 refers to the last output, in this case the output \
produces by computing the difference ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(30!\) - \(12!\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[".  Using  ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["%",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["  is often more convenient than making assignments, and is also \
useful to display intermediate results in pipes. ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\((\(-a\) - b)\)\^10\), "\n", 
    \(Expand[%]\), "\n", 
    \(Factor[%]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Also, if you refer to the explicit ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Out[n]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-number of a result, you can use it in other input as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(%\ n\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Lists", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The single most important data structure in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["list",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". The elements of a list can be absolutely anything: integers, reals, \
equations, rules, other lists\[LongDash]whatever. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["WARNING. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "Lists in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are denoted with ",
  StyleBox["curly braces",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", perhaps a somewhat unfortunate choice, since in traditional mathematics \
and computer science the same notation is used for ",
  StyleBox["sets",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". But on the standard keyboard, once ( and ) and [ and ] have been used \
for other purposes, only { and } are left as brackets.  But keep in mind that \
",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " often uses lists ",
  StyleBox["in place of",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " sets.  The key difference between a list and a set is that the terms of a \
list occur ",
  StyleBox["in order",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and with possible ",
  StyleBox["repetitions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  In other words, don't not confuse the list ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \({a, b, a, b}\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "with the list ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \({b, a}\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", even though the sets are the same",
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 1: Selecting parts of lists", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To ",
  StyleBox["select",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a term from a given list, you can use the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(list\[LeftDoubleBracket]n\[RightDoubleBracket]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "-notation, which can be entered by typing [[ and ]]:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \({a, b, c, d, e}\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \({a, b, c, d, 
        e}\[LeftDoubleBracket]4\[RightDoubleBracket]\), \
"\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \({a, b, c, d, e}\[LeftDoubleBracket]{2, 
        4}\[RightDoubleBracket]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 2: More operations on lists", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There are countless ",
  StyleBox["built-in ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "operations on lists.  Here are some examples."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(L = Range[10]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
L // First
L // Rest
L // Last
L // Reverse
L // Reverse // Last
L // RotateLeft
L // RotateRight\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 3: Arithmetic on lists", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note, too, that a lot of the basic operations in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " automatically ",
  StyleBox["extend",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to lists:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(L\^2\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\((\(-2\)\ L)\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(3\ L\  + 1\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 4: Matrices", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Lists of lists",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can be used to implement ",
  StyleBox["matrices",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(hilbert = Table[1\/\(i + j - 1\), {i, 6}, {j, 6}];\)\), "\n", 
    \(hilbert\), "\n", 
    \(MatrixForm[hilbert]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Det[hilbert]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(g = Inverse[hilbert];\)\), "\n", 
    \(MatrixForm[g]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatrixForm[hilbert . g]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 5: Sorting lists", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Lists can also be ",
  StyleBox["sorted",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (but it takes some effort to understand what ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " means by the ",
  StyleBox["canonical order",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", especially for symbolic expressions):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Sort[{4, 1\/3, \@2, \(-9\), 1.5, a, {a, b}, \@2\ a, a\^2\ b\^c, 
        a\ b + c, a\^b\ c}]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Graphics and sound", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Two and three-dimensional graphics commands make it easy to ",
  StyleBox["visualize data",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". There are only a few basic commands to learn, but in order to fine tune \
the pictures one can use dozens of so-called ",
  StyleBox["Options",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ".  Get help on the key-word ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["Options",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to see some possibilities. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  " Fine tuning graphics affords a great opportunity to waste many hours!"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Another Warning.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  " Intensive use of graphics in a notebook can often cause memory-related \
crashes of your computer.  If the pictures and other output are not needed at \
some point in reading a notebook, then the Kernel-menu command ",
  StyleBox["Delete All Output",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " can clear away the old results.  It is often advisable to do this same \
deletion before ",
  StyleBox["saving",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a notebook, provided that all results can be easily recreated by running \
the commands in the notebook the next time it is opened."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Libraries", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Most graphics commands are built into ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", but for some special commands you need to load ",
  StyleBox["special libraries",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The contents of these libraries can be listed via the Help Browser.  \
The ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Needs\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-",
  "command ",
  StyleBox["loads",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " libraries.  Execute the ones below in order to be able to run the \
subsequent examples."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Needs["\<Graphics`Graphics`\>"]\), "\n", 
    \(Needs["\<Graphics`Colors`\>"]\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" 2-dimensional plotting", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 1: Plotting primes", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "First, a simple",
  StyleBox[" plotting of lists of integers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(primes = Table[Prime[i], {i, 50}]\), "\n", 
    \(\(ListPlot[primes, PlotStyle \[Rule] Red];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The list plot above is written in ",
  StyleBox["user-friendly form ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "(",
  StyleBox["i.e., understandable form",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "). ",
  " A hacker might type something like this:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["50 // Range // Prime // ListPlot;", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "More dramatic pictures can be made with ",
  StyleBox["bar charts",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell["BarChart[Take[primes,20]];", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["BarChart[Take[primes,{20,30}]];", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Or for a different style of chart:", "Text"],

Cell["PieChart[Take[primes,20]];", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["PieChart[Take[primes,{20,30}]];", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 2: Counting Digits", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is a plot of the number of 1's in the binary expansion of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`n\)]],
  ", where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`1\  \[LessEqual] \ n\  \[LessEqual] \ 512\)]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(dig = \((Plus @@ IntegerDigits[#1, 2] &)\) /@ 
          Range[512];\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(ListPlot[dig, PlotStyle \[Rule] Blue];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["A barchart may make the pattern easier to see:", "Text"],

Cell["BarChart[Take[dig,40]];", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " also contains the built-in function ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["DigitCount",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", which could also have been used in this example."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?DigitCount\)\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 3: Pascal's triangle", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "A popular type of  ",
  StyleBox["fractal image ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "that displays the geometry of ",
  StyleBox["binomial coefficients",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " using some graphics options can be obtained as shown in these plots. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(tab = 
        Reverse[1 - 
            Table[Mod[Binomial[i, j], 2], {i, 100}, {j, 100}]];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Show[
        Graphics[Raster[tab], AspectRatio \[Rule] 1, Frame \[Rule] True, 
          FrameTicks \[Rule] None]];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(tab = 
        Reverse[1\/2\ Table[
              2 - Mod[Binomial[i, j], 3], {i, 0, 64}, {j, 0, 
                64}]];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Show[Graphics[Raster[tab], AspectRatio \[Rule] 1]];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ListContourPlot[tab]; \)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 4: Fractions and denominators", "Example"],

Cell["\<\
Here is another picture related to the behavior of fractions in \
elementary arithmetic. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(tab = 
        Flatten[Table[
            Point[{p\/q, Denominator[p\/q]}], {q, 100}, {p, 
              q - 1}]];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Show[
        Graphics[Join[{PointSize[0.007], RGBColor[1, 0, 0]}, tab], 
          Frame \[Rule] True]];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 5: Plotting curves", "Example"],

Cell["Finally, here are some plots from calculus. ", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plot[Sin[x] + Sin[\@x], {x, 0, 100}]; \)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot[\[ExponentialE]\^\(-x\^2\)\ Sin[
            10\ x], {x, \(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi]}];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot[{Sin[5\ x], Sin[6\ x]}, {x, 0, 
          2\ \[Pi]}];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot[{ChebyshevT[6, x], ChebyshevT[11, x]}, {x, \(-1\), 1}, 
        PlotStyle \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.01], Red}, {Thickness[0.015], 
              Purple}}];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "By the way, pictures like the above can be ",
  StyleBox["magnified",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by selecting the graphics area and then ",
  StyleBox["dragging",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a side or corner."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 6: Beauty from chaos", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox[" And we cannot resist some \"random art\".",
  FontWeight->"Plain"]], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(circs = 
        Table[{Hue[Random[]], 
            Circle[{2\ Random[], 3\ Random[]}, 
              Random[]\^1\/2]}, {60}];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Show[Graphics[circs], AspectRatio \[Rule] Automatic, 
        Frame \[Rule] True, FrameTicks \[Rule] None];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" 3-dimensional plotting", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 1: Plotting surfaces", "Example"],

Cell["Here are some interesting surfaces in 3-dimensional space. ", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plot3D[Sin[x\ y], {x, 0, 4}, {y, 0, 4}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 50]; \)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot3D[\(x\ y\)\/\(x\^2 + y\^2\), {x, \(-1\), 1}, {y, \(-1\), 1}, 
        PlotPoints \[Rule] 60];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note.",
  StyleBox["  Since there is a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["singularity",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" at the origin, the plot has some trouble with the drawing of the \
surface.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 2: Viewing a surface", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Doubly periodic functions make pleasant surfaces.",
  FontWeight->"Plain"]], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot3D[
        Cos[x]\ Sin[3\ y], {x, \(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi]}, {y, \(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi]}, 
        Lighting \[Rule] True, PlotPoints \[Rule] 40];\)\)], "Input",
  PageBreakAbove->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can then look at the surface from a ",
  StyleBox["different angle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Show[%, ViewPoint \[Rule] {1, 2, 2}];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
We can also get contour levels and density plots for this \
surface.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ContourPlot[
        Cos[x]\ Sin[3\ y], {x, \(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi]}, {y, \(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi]}, \ 
        PlotPoints \[Rule] 100];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 3: Coloring contours", "Example"],

Cell["\<\
Painting colors on a surface help to make the \"hills and valleys\" \
of a function better visible.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ContourPlot[
        Sin[x]\ Sin[y], {x, \(-2\) \[Pi], 2  \[Pi]}, {y, \(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi]}, 
        ColorFunction \[Rule] Hue, \ PlotPoints \[Rule] 100];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 4: Density plots", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["The use of a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["density plot",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" can help to understand a surface better.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Plot3D[Sin[1\/\(x\ y\)], {x, \(-1.5\), 1.5}, {y, \(-1.5\), 1.5}, 
        Mesh \[Rule] False, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(DensityPlot[Sin[1\/\(x\ y\)], {x, \(-1.5\), 1.5}, {y, \(-1.5\), 1.5}, 
        Mesh \[Rule] False, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
This function has faster and faster fluctuations in approaching the \
axes.\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 5: Arrays of plots", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["It is also possible to display several related \
figures together.",
  FontWeight->"Plain"]], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f[x_, y_] := Sin[\(x\ y\)\/4];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(surf = 
        Plot3D[f[x, y], {x, \(-4\), 4}, {y, \(-4\), 4}, 
          DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(mesh = 
        Plot3D[f[x, y], {x, \(-4\), 4}, {y, \(-4\), 4}, 
          DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity, HiddenSurface \[Rule] False, 
          MeshStyle \[Rule] {RGBColor[1, 0, 0], 
              Thickness[0.005]}];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(cont = 
        ContourPlot[f[x, y], {x, \(-4\), 4}, {y, \(-4\), 4}, 
          ColorFunction \[Rule] Hue, 
          DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(dens = 
        DensityPlot[f[x, y], {x, \(-4\), 4}, {y, \(-4\), 4}, 
          ColorFunction \[Rule] Hue, 
          DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Show[GraphicsArray[{{surf, mesh}, {dens, cont}}], 
        DisplayFunction \[Rule] $DisplayFunction];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Polygonal and Parametric 3D", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 1: Random polygons", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Various ",
  StyleBox["polygons",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can be drawn easily from the built-in primitives.  We experiment here \
with using ",
  StyleBox["random numbers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ranpoly := Polygon[Table[Random[], {3}, {3}]]\), "\n", 
    \(\(Show[Graphics3D[Table[ranpoly, {5}]]];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note.",
  StyleBox["  Every time you run this command, you get a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["different",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" picture.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["More polygons give more structure.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Show[Graphics3D[Table[ranpoly, {10}]]];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Question.",
  StyleBox["  Is this ART?",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(tab = 
        Flatten[Table[
            If[Mod[Multinomial[x, y, z], 2] == 1, 
              Cuboid[1.2\ {x, y, \(-z\)}], {}], {x, 0, 15}, {y, 0, 15}, {z, 
              0, 15}]];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Show[Graphics3D[tab]];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 2: ParametricPlot3D", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can plot ",
  StyleBox["parametric curves and surfaces",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in three dimensions. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot3D[{u\ Sin[\[Pi]\ u], u\ Cos[\[Pi]\ u], u}, {u, 0, 20}, 
        DefaultColor \[Rule] RGBColor[1, 0, 0], 
        BoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 1}, \[IndentingNewLine]PlotPoints \[Rule] 
          300];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot3D[{Sin[u]\ Cos[v], Sin[u]\ Sin[v], Cos[u]}, {u, 
          0, \[Pi]}, {v, 0, \[Pi]\/2}, 
        BoxRatios \[Rule] {1, 1, 1}];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot3D[{v\ Cos[u], v\ Sin[u], u\/3}, {u, 0, 
          5\ \[Pi]}, {v, \(-2\), 2}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 80];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 3: Plotting a torus", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Lastly, the inevitable ",
  StyleBox["donut",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot3D[{\((1 + Cos[u]\/2)\)\ Cos[
              v], \((1 + Cos[u]\/2)\)\ Sin[v], 
          Sin[u]\/2}, {u, \(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi]}, {v, \(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi]}, \ 
        PlotPoints \[Rule] 50];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we can ",
  StyleBox["skew",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " it by changing a factor."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ParametricPlot3D[{\((1 + Cos[u]\/2)\)\ Cos[
              v], \((1 + Cos[u]\/10)\)\ Sin[v], 
          Sin[u]\/2}, {u, \(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi]}, {v, \(-\[Pi]\), \[Pi]}, \ 
        PlotPoints \[Rule] 50];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Koch Snowflake Curve", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This and the following example show that it is quite straightforward to \
generate graphics that illustrate relatively complicated objects such as ",
  StyleBox["fractals",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  In either case, don't worry about the code yet, just run it."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(Clear[dist, unit, Koch, KochStep, polLength];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(dist[{{x1_, x2_}, {y1_, y2_}}] := 
        N[\@\(\((x1 - y1)\)\^2 + \((x2 - y2)\)\^2\)];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(polLength[k_List] := 
        Plus @@ \(dist /@ Partition[k, 2, 1]\);\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(unit[x_List] := N[x\/dist[{{0, 0}, x}]];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Koch[{}] = {{0, 0}, {1, 0}};\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Koch[LL_List] := 
        Join[{First[LL]}, 
          Flatten[KochStep /@ Partition[LL, 2, 1], 1]];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(KochStep[{a_, b_}] := 
        Block[{a1, a2, a3, x, x0, nv}, x = b - a; x0 = unit[x]; 
          nv = {\(-x0\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]\), 
              x0\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]}; a1 = a + x\/3; 
          a2 = a + x\/2 + N[1\/5\ dist[{a, b}]\ \@3]\ nv; 
          a3 = a + \(2\ x\)\/3; N[{a1, a2, a3, b}]];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["We compute a list of the first 5 Koch curves. ", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(curves = Rest[NestList[Koch, {}, 6]];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
The lengths of the curves increase by a constant multiplicative \
factor, approximately 1.43, so the limiting curve will have unbounded length. \
\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(len = polLength /@ curves\), "\n", 
    \(len\ len\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["And here is the pictures.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(gr = \((Show[
                Graphics[{Blue, Polygon[#1], Red, Line[{{0, 0}, {1, 0}}]}], 
                PlotRange \[Rule] {{0, 1}, {0,  .6}}, 
                AspectRatio \[Rule] Automatic] &)\) /@ curves;\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Smith/Sierpinski Gasket", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
The following example is usually attribute to Sierpinski, though \
Henry J.S. Smith at Oxford found it a number of years earlier. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[next, DropDrop, toSquare]\), "\n", 
    \(\(DropDrop[xx_, pos_] := Fold[Drop[#1, {#2}] &, xx, pos];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(\(next[mm_, kk_]\)[{{x0_, x1_}, {y0_, y1_}}] := 
        DropDrop[
          Distribute[{Partition[x0 + \(\((x1 - x0)\)\ Range[0, mm]\)\/mm, 2, 
                1], Partition[y0 + \(\((y1 - y0)\)\ Range[0, mm]\)\/mm, 2, 
                1]}, List], kk];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(\(next[mm_, kk_]\)[LL_List] := 
        Flatten[next[mm, kk] /@ LL, 1];\)\), "\n", 
    \(toSquare[{{x0_, x1_}, {y0_, y1_}}] := 
      Rectangle[{x0, y0}, {x1, y1}]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(next[3, {5}]\)[{{0. , 1}, {0. , 1}}]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Here is the first picture.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(gr = 
        toSquare /@ Nest[next[3, {5}], {{0. , 1}, {0. , 1}}, 4];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Show[
        Graphics[Join[{RGBColor[0, 0, 1]}, gr], AspectRatio \[Rule] 1, 
          Frame \[Rule] True, FrameTicks \[Rule] None]];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Here is a different picture.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(gr = 
        toSquare /@ 
          Nest[next[3, {3, 8}], {{0. , 1}, {0. , 1}}, 4];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Show[
        Graphics[Join[{Red}, gr], AspectRatio \[Rule] 1, Frame \[Rule] True, 
          FrameTicks \[Rule] None]];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
We can animate the sequence of approximations produced by the next \
piece of code.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Do[gr = toSquare /@ Nest[next[2, {4}], {{0. , 1}, {0. , 1}}, i]; 
      Show[Graphics[Join[{RGBColor[0, 0, 1]}, gr], AspectRatio \[Rule] 1, 
          Frame \[Rule] True, FrameTicks \[Rule] None]], {i, 5}]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Or, we can overlay the pictures in different colors. ", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(nn = 6;\)\), "\n", 
    \(Do[gr = toSquare /@ Nest[next[2, {4}], {{0. , 1}, {0. , 1}}, i]; 
      pic[i] = Show[
          Graphics[Join[{Hue[i\/nn]}, gr], AspectRatio \[Rule] 1, 
            Frame \[Rule] True, FrameTicks \[Rule] None], 
          DisplayFunction \[Rule] Identity], {i, nn}]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Show[ pic /@ Range[nn], DisplayFunction->$DisplayFunction ];", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Here are the colors being used in this last picture.", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
RasterArray[ {Hue[#/nn]& /@ Range[nn]} ] // Graphics // Show;\
\>", \
"Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Adding dynamics", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 1: A simple animation", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can make \"moving pictures\".  After running the next command, close the \
bracket on the plots so that only one picture is visible."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Do[Plot[Sin[n\ x], {x, 0, 2\ \[Pi]}], {n, 1, 5}]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, select the bracket, and from the ",
  StyleBox["Cell",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " menu, choose ",
  StyleBox["Animate Selected Graphics",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 2: More complicated animations", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Do the same as in Example 1, but type ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "<",
  StyleBox[" to slow the display of the frames down.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Do[ParametricPlot[{Cos[n\ t]\ Sin[t], Cos[n\ t]\ Cos[t]}, {t, 0, 
          2\ \[Pi]}, AspectRatio \[Rule] Automatic, Ticks \[Rule] None], {n, 
        2, 8, 1}]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Do[ParametricPlot[{Cos[n\ t]\ Sin[11\ t], Cos[11\ t]\ Cos[n\ t]}, {t, 
          0, 2\ \[Pi]}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, 
        PlotStyle \[Rule] RGBColor[0, 0, 1], AspectRatio \[Rule] 1, 
        Ticks \[Rule] None], {n, 1, 10, 1}]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Sound Generation", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Musical sounds are ",
  StyleBox["periodic vibrations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in the air that can be analyzed in terms of combinations of ",
  StyleBox["sine waves",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". In ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the Play-command combined with a sine function will give suitable \
results\[LongDash]if provided with the appropriate ",
  StyleBox["frequency",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  For example, would you like an \"A\" to tune your violin?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Play[Sin[2\ \[Pi]\ 440\ t], {t, 0, 2}];\)\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(Play[Sin[4\ \[Pi]\ 220\ t], {t, 0, 1}];\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(Play[Sin[5\ \[Pi]\ 220\ t], {t, 0, 1}];\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(Play[Sin[6\ \[Pi]\ 220\ t], {t, 0, 1}];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Play[Sin[10000\/t], {t, \(-4\), 4}]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A look at the Calculus", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Integration and Differentiation", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 1: Definite and indefinite integrals", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The integration algorithm covers most of the known kinds of ",
  StyleBox["integrals",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". You can calculate ",
  StyleBox["definite",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as well as ",
  StyleBox["indefinite",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " integrals.   "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["WARNING. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  " You may type from the keyboard ",
  StyleBox["Integrate[x^n, x]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", but you will see a more usual and more readable mathematical notation.  \
In input-cells the mathematical notation can be displayed by a ",
  StyleBox["Command-N",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ", while the input format can be found by ",
  StyleBox["Command-I",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\[Integral]\(x\^n\) \[DifferentialD]x\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\[Integral]\(Sin[x]\ Cos[x]\) \[DifferentialD]x\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\[Integral]\_a\%b\( x\^5\) \[DifferentialD]x\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 2: Improper integrals", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Improper",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" integrals can also be computed.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\[Integral]\_1\%\[Infinity]\( 1\/x\) \[DifferentialD]x\), "\n", 
    \(\[Integral]\_1\%\[Infinity]\( 1\/x\^2\) \[DifferentialD]x\), "\n", 
    \(\[Integral]\_1\%\[Infinity]\( 1\/x\^3\) \[DifferentialD]x\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["And note that sometimes warning messages are generated.", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 3: TraditionalForm", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that there are ",
  StyleBox["two formats ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "for output."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Integrate[ 1/(1 + x^5), x ] // StandardForm", "Input"],

Cell["Integrate[ 1/(1 + x^5), x ] // TraditionalForm", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Most of time, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(StandardForm\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is adequate, but for publication, the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["TraditionalForm",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " may be more readable."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 4: Special functions and constants", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Some of the answers given by the built-in integration algorithm require \
quite a bit of ",
  StyleBox["real analysis",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to understand. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\[Integral]\_0\%\[Infinity]\( Log[
            x]\ \[ExponentialE]\^\(-x\^2\)\) \[DifferentialD]x\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\[Integral]\_0\%\[Infinity]\( Sin[
            x\^2]\ \[ExponentialE]\^\(-x\)\) \[DifferentialD]x\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 5: Verifying an integral", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can ",
  StyleBox["verify",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the integration results by differentiating."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ff = 
      x\ \[ExponentialE]\^x\ Sin[x] - 
        1\/2\ \[ExponentialE]\^x\ Cos[x]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(FF = \[Integral]ff \[DifferentialD]x\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
To check equality, we use the standard trick of calculating the \
difference:\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\[PartialD]\_x FF - ff\), "\n", 
    \(Simplify[%]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 6: Verifying another integral", "Example"],

Cell["Here is another verification.", "Text",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ff = 1\/\((a + b\ x\^2)\)\^2\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(FF = \[Integral]ff \[DifferentialD]x\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Simplify[\[PartialD]\_x FF - ff]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example 7: Differential equations", "Example"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Differential equations ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "can also be solved ",
  StyleBox["numerically",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{\(Clear[x, t]\), "\n", 
    RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"sol", "=", 
        RowBox[{"NDSolve", "[", 
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"{", 
              RowBox[{
                RowBox[{
                  RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{
                      SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Prime]\[Prime]",
                        MultilineFunction->None], "[", "t", "]"}], 
                    "+", \(x[t]\^3\)}], "==", \(Cos[t]\)}], ",", 
                RowBox[{\(x[0]\), "==", 
                  RowBox[{
                    SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Prime]",
                      MultilineFunction->None], "[", "0", "]"}], "==", 
                  "0"}]}], "}"}], ",", "x", ",", \({t, 0, 30}\), 
            ",", \(MaxSteps \[Rule] 600\)}], "]"}]}], ";"}]}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can display both the solution and its derivative ",
  StyleBox["separately",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{\(ParametricPlot[
        Evaluate[{t, x[t]} /. \[InvisibleSpace]sol], {t, 0, 30}, 
        PlotStyle \[Rule] RGBColor[0, 0, 1]];\), "\n", 
    RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"ParametricPlot", "[", 
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Evaluate", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"{", 
                RowBox[{"t", ",", 
                  RowBox[{
                    SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Prime]",
                      MultilineFunction->None], "[", "t", "]"}]}], "}"}], "/.",
               "\[InvisibleSpace]", "sol"}], "]"}], ",", \({t, 0, 30}\), 
          ",", \(PlotStyle \[Rule] RGBColor[0, 0, 1]\)}], "]"}], 
      ";"}]}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Or we can display them ",
  StyleBox["jointly",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in a 2-dimensional parametric plot."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"ParametricPlot", "[", 
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Evaluate", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"{", 
                RowBox[{\(x[t]\), ",", 
                  RowBox[{
                    SuperscriptBox["x", "\[Prime]",
                      MultilineFunction->None], "[", "t", "]"}]}], "}"}], "/.",
               "\[InvisibleSpace]", "sol"}], "]"}], ",", \({t, 0, 30}\), 
          ",", \(PlotStyle \[Rule] RGBColor[1, 0, 0]\)}], "]"}], 
      ";"}]], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Limits", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Mathematica can calculate certain limits, though one usually has to \
perform some simplifications and transformations by hand to get interesting \
results. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Limit[ a/x, x->Infinity ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["t = Log[Cos[x]]/x^2", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
t /. x -> 0
t /. x -> 0.1
t /. x -> 0.01
t /. x -> 0.001\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\

Limit[t,x -> 0]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Limit[Sin[x]/x,x->0]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Limit[Sin[Pi x]/(x^2-2 x+1),x->1]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Limit[Exp[1/x],x->Infinity]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Series", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can perform Taylor expansions with the built-in command ",
  StyleBox["Series",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". The output consists of a few terms of the series, plus an error term of \
the form  O(..). The latter can be chopped off with ",
  StyleBox["Normal",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Series[ Sin[x], {x,-1,10} ]
% // Normal // ExpandAll
% // N\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Some infinite series can be summed numerically (but not directly \
symbolically). \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["NSum[ (1/5)^i, {i,0,Infinity} ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["NSum[ i^4/i!, {i,1,Infinity} ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Programming Language"
}], "Section",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " contains a large number of fairly complicated built-in functions, a great \
many problems can be tackled by just typing in a command, or a short sequence \
of commands (one-liners).  On occasion, however, it is necessary to develop \
small ",
  StyleBox["programs",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". For this purpose there is a complete programming language that comes \
along with the system. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Indeed, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " supports a number of different programming ",
  StyleBox["styles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", giving the user considerable leeway on how a given problem may be \
solved. Of course, one may also get a little confused by all the different \
possibilities. If you have prior programming experience in a procedural \
language like Pascal or C, you will probably find that your first ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " programs look a lot like Pascal or C programs \[LongDash] and are \
inefficient as a result.  Later, your style will probably become more ",
  StyleBox["functional",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["rule-based",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "At any rate, the notebook environment makes it very easy to experiment \
with the ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " language: type in a piece of code, execute, see what output it produces, \
modify it, and so forth. In the end, after testing an approach to the \
solution of a problem, a sequence of code segments can be assembled into a \
larger program. This process is quite a bit less painful than the usual \
edit-compile-debug cycle in traditional programming languages.  You just have \
to get used to a different way of writing code. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A Warning", "Subsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Language purists be warned:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  " the ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " programming language is in many ways a ",
  StyleBox["hodge-podge",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  It was not meant to demonstrate any particular approach to programming, \
and consequently is not exactly a model of coherence and consistency.  Some \
of its features can be quite infuriating.  Speaking from experience, though, \
we can assure you that it does get the job done.  It is even possible to \
build fairly large and complicated software systems around ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", for example, take a look at Sutner's home page for a system dealing with \
",
  StyleBox["automata theory",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (http://www.cs.cmu/~sutner). "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Parentheses, Brackets and Braces", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
The conventions in Mathematica for the use of parentheses, brackets \
and braces are somewhat different from their ordinary use in mathematics and \
computer science. Beware.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Parentheses:  Grouping", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
The only use of parentheses in Mathematica is for grouping. They \
are also used for the same purpose in mathematics and computer science. \
\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["1/(a + 1)", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
(a+b)^3
% // Expand\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Brackets: Function Application", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Brackets are used for function application and delimit the \
arguments to the function call.  In mathematics and computer science \
parentheses are the standard choice here.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Sin[Pi/3]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You cannot write ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Sin \((Pi/3)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(sin \((Pi/3)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and so forth, but at least there is a warning message. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["sin(Pi/3)", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Unfortunately, the designers of Mathematica decided to use (double) \
brackets for another important operation: extraction of parts of an \
expression. The double brackets correspond to the usual array access \
operation in languages such as Pascal and C.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
{a,b,c}[[1]]
{a,b,c}[[-1]]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["But not", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["{a,b,c}[1]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Braces: Lists", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Curly braces are used for lists. In mathematics and computer \
science braces are often used to denote sets, i.e., unordered lists without \
repetition.  Lists in Mathematica are always ordered, and repetitions are \
allowed.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
{a, b, c, c}
Range[10]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Lists can be nested in an arbitrary way, and can contain elements \
of different types. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["L = {a, b, {123,456}, a, a, {{a},{{b}}}, c }", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Recall the double bracket notation for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Part\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " which allows us to access pieces of a list. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
L[[-2]]
L[[-2,2]]
L[[-2,2,1]]
L[[-2,2,1,1]]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Basic Control Structures", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Mathematica supports all the basic control structures that one would \
expect from a higher level language. Careful, though, the syntax is sometimes \
a bit surprising.  In particular watch out for commas and semicolons.  It is \
a good idea to look up the top level topic ",
  StyleBox["Programming",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " in the help browser.  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here are some of the basic control structures in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica:",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(x = expr;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(Clear[x]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(If[cond, statement]\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(If[cond, statement1, statement2]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Switch[exp, pat1, val1, pat2, val2,  ... ]\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(Which[test1, val1, test2, val2,  ... ]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(While[cond, statement]\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(Do[statement1, iter]\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(For[start, stop, inc, statement]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(With[{xx = expr}, statement]\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(Module[{v1, v2,  ... }, statement]\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Built-ins", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Many tasks can be handled directly using built-in functions. It is outside \
the scope of this course to give even an overview of what can be done \
directly in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica,",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " consult the manual or the help browser. Here is just one example that \
will be used further down for comparison. \nConsider the task of summing the \
square root of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`x\)]],
  ", where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`x\  = \ 1, 2, ...\( , 100\)\)]],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Sum[ N[Sqrt[i]], {i,100} ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["NSum[ Sqrt[i], {i,100} ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(NSum[f, {x, i}]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " performs the same computation as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(N[Sum[f, {x, i}]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  If you forget to include the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(N\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", though, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " will give you an exact result."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Sum[ Sqrt[i], {i,100} ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
You can make the sum look very fancy, but this will not be a major \
concern to us. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[\ \[Sum]\_\(i = 1\)\%100\@ i\ ]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Procedural Programming", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Although the built-in function ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Sum\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can be used to sum ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\@x\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(x = 1\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(2\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", \[Ellipsis], ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(100\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", this operation can also be performed with explicit loops.  Here it is \
with a ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(For\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " loop, a ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Do\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " loop, and a ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(While\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " loop."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sum = 0;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(For[i = 1, i \[LessEqual] 100, \(i++\), 
        sum = sum + N[Sqrt[i]]];\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(sum\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sum = 0;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(Do[sum = sum + N[Sqrt[i]], {i, 100}];\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(sum\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(sum = 0;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(x = 1;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(While[x \[LessEqual] 100, 
        sum = sum + 
            N[Sqrt[x]]; \[IndentingNewLine]\(x++\)];\)\), \
"\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(sum\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Of course, the explicit loops are slower than the implicit one in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Sum\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note the use of parentheses to collect the three statements into one, \
which is then handed over to the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Timing\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " command. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
( sum = 0; For[ i=1, i<=10000, i++, sum = sum + i ]; sum ) // \
Timing\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Sum[ i, {i,10000} ] // Timing", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Functional Programming", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For many mathematical manipulations it turns out that functions are \
crucially important.  Some languages therefore provide functions as \
fundamental data types that can be manipulated in various ways. In the \
following example, the function that turns  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`n\)]],
  "  into the numerical value of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\@n\)]],
  "  is applied to all numbers from 1 to 100, and the results are then added \
up. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Apply[ Plus, Map[ N[Sqrt[#]]&, Range[100] ] ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
This looks very cryptic, but read the explanations below and you \
will see that it is actually quite simple, and even natural.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["The first 100 prime numbers can be generated thus: ", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Map[ Prime, Range[100] ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["The more traditional approach would be to do something like", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
primes = {};
For[ i = 1, i <= 100, i++, 
\t\tAppendTo[ primes, Prime[i] ] 
];
primes\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As already mentioned, functions are important in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". There are countless built-in functions (which we will refer to as \
commands), but the user can add her own. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
By the way, the square root  function knows how to deal with lists \
of numbers as input:\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Sqrt[Range[10]]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is true for any function that is ",
  StyleBox["listable",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  You can check if a function is listable with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(?? \)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(?? Sqrt\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Listable\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the first \"attribute\" for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["Sqrt",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " that is given in the output."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Named Functions", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Dummy variables used in function definitions are indicated by an \
underscore, called ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Blank[]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in Mathematica. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[f]\), "\n", 
    \(f[x_] := x\^2 + 5\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that nothing is returned, no value has been computed. The ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[":=",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is completely different from the assignment operator ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \( = \)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  However, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " now remembers that the symbol ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(f\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is associated with a definition. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["??f", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["We can now use that definition to perform calculations.", "Text"],

Cell["f[3]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And since ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can deal with symbolic entities, the input need not be an integer."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
f[a]
f[Sqrt[2]]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["WARNING:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "  Whenever you define a function, always clear the corresponding symbol \
first.  Mathematica is rather stubborn in holding on to information.  All \
kinds of bizarre problems can occur when you do not to clear symbols."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
Here is the proper way to define a function of two variables.\
\>", \
"Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Clear[g]
g[x_,y_] := x^2 + 5 x y - y^2;
Plot3D[ g[x,y], {x,-1,1}, {y,-1,1} ]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Often it is very convenient to have functions with a variable number of \
arguments.  One can indicate a sequence of dummy variables by typing two \
underscores.  Actually, two underscores indicate any sequence of length at \
least 1, if we wish to allow the empty sequence we have to use three \
underscores. This is our first encounter with ",
  StyleBox["patterns",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", you will hear more about patterns shortly."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Clear[mysum]
mysum[ xx__, y_ ] := Plus[xx]/y\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["mysum[1,2,a,5]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To get some idea of what is going on in the evaluation of the last \
expression, we can use the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Trace\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " function.  Be careful, on larger calculations ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Trace\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can produce enormous amounts of output. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Trace[ mysum[1,2,a,5] ]", "Input"],

Cell["mysum[ 5, 2 ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["mysum[ 3 ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We need at least two inputs for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(mysum\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to evaluate.  We can fix this problem by adding another underscore. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Clear[mysum]
mysum[ xx___,y_ ] := Plus[xx]/y\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["mysum[ 3 ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Of course, our fix hinges on the reasonable convention that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Plus[]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Plus[]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "An analogous convention holds for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Times\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Times[]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Map", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
One often finds oneself in a position where a function has to be \
evaluated on a whole range of arguments. The arguments can be collected into \
a list, and we can get the values, again as a list, by doing something like \
\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Clear[f]
A = {1,2,3,a,b};
B = {};

For[ i=1, i<=Length[A], i++, 
\tAppendTo[ B, f[ A[[i]] ] ]
];
B\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Alternatively, the built-in command ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Map\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " could be used.  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Map\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " automatically takes care of the loop and all the list manipulations. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Map[ f, {1,2,3,a,b} ]", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
So, if we wanted to produce a list of the first 50 squares we could \
simply do the following. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Clear[f]
f[x_] := x^2;
Map[ f, Range[50] ]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Map\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can also be abbreviated by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["/@",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["f /@ Range[50]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Apply", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Suppose we have a function ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(f\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " of, say, two arguments, and we have a list of arguments ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(list = {a, b}\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  If we wish to calculate ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \({f[a], f[b]}\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then simply typing ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(f[list]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " will not suffice unless ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(f\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is listable (see above)."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[f]\), "\n", 
    \(f[x_, y_] := x + y; \), "\n", 
    \(f[{3, 5}]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(f\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " expects two arguments, but it receives only one, namely a list.  We can \
fix this problem by replacing the ordinary function call (indicated by square \
brackets) with the command ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Apply\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Apply", "SmallText"],
  "[ f, {3,5} ]"
}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Apply\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can also be abbreviated by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["@@",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["f@@{3,5}", "SmallText"]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Although you may choose not to use this abbreviation, you will encounter \
it when you use functions such as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Trace\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Trace[Apply", "SmallText"],
  "[ f, {3,5} ]]"
}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Apply\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " command is not restricted to lists\[LongDash]it also works on any other \
kind of expression."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Clear[g,a,b]
Apply[ f, g[a,b] ]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell["The trace shows how g is replaced by f. ", "Text"],

Cell["Apply[ f, g[a,b] ]  // Trace", "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Anonymous Functions", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If a function is to be used only once, there is no point in attaching a \
name to it. You can use the command ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Function\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to define a function:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Function[xx, xx\^2 + 1]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(xx\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the dummy variable, and the definition is pretty clear. This object \
behaves just the way a function should.  If you are familiar with LISP (or \
the \[Lambda]-calculus), this is precisely the idea of lambda expressions. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Function[xx, xx\^2 + 1]\)[100]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
You can apply this function to all the numbers 1 through 10 as \
follows.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[Function[xx, xx\^2 + 1], Range[10]]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Function\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can also be used to define functions of more than one argument."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Function[{xx, yy, zz}, \[IndentingNewLine]xx\^2 + yy\^3 + zz\^4 + 
          1]\)[50, \(-23\), 10]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Like many other commands, there are abbreviations that can be used with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Function\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  First, you can delete the dummy variable and use the default dummy ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(#\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Function[#\^2 + 1]\)[100]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If the function has more than one argument, then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(#1\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(#2\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(#3\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and so on may be used to represent the dummy variables."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Function[#1\^2 + #2\^3 + #3\^4 + 1]\)[50, \(-23\), 10]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Second, you may delete the header ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Function\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and replace it with an ampersand ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["&",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at the end.  This notation may take some getting used to, but it is a \
little easier to type than the full form. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\((#\^2 + 1 &)\)\), "\n", 
    \(\((#\^2 + 1 &)\)[100]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This can be used in combination with other commands, such as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Map\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[\((#\^2 + 1 &)\), Range[10]]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Recall that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Map\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can be abbreviated with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["/@",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["(#^2 + 1)& /@ Range[10]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
We will try to avoid this sort of syntax, but you should be aware \
of its existence.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Note that functions, like everything else, can also be assigned to \
variables. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["f = (#^2 + 1)&", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["f /@ Range[10]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Manipulating Functions", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Mathematica has a number of commands that manipulate functions directly. \
Other than ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Map\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", the most important ones are ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Nest\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Fold\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  The command ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Nest\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " allows one to apply a function repeatedly to an argument. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[f]\), "\n", 
    \(Nest[f, a, 5]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Repeated function application is a standard mathematical tool for \
the approximation of numbers. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Nest[1/\((1 + #)\) &, 1, 10]\), "\n", 
    \(N[Nest[1/\((1 + #)\) &, 1, 50]]\), "\n", 
    \(N[1/GoldenRatio - %]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There is also a command to compute fixed points (i.e., values ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`x\)]],
  " such that  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`f(x)\  = \ x\)]],
  ") of functions. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["FixedPoint[N[1/(1 + #)]&, 1]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["N[1/GoldenRatio] == %", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Be careful with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(FixedPoint\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", though.  It can easily go into an infinite loop if there is no fixed \
point. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Fold\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " also nests a function, but this time a function of two arguments. There \
are many examples where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Fold\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " pops up naturally."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[p, f]\), "\n", 
    \(Fold[f, p, Range[5]]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For example, we can produce a continued fraction with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Fold\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Clear[a]
Fold[ (#2 + 1/#1)&, a, {4,1,1,2,1,2}]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["List-based Programming", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The last example shows that functional programming style can make things \
much more clear.  Often, functional programming goes hand in hand with \
operations on lists.  For example, one applies a function to all the elements \
in a list, and obtains a list of results.  In ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", list manipulations are crucial, and many built-in commands were designed \
specifically to make use of lists."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["To get the list of the first 100 primes we can simply write:", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Prime[ Range[100] ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Arithmetic also extends to lists naturally.  Suppose we have to \
compute the forward differences between the first 20 squares.  The procedural \
way is something along the lines of:\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(dif = {};\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(For[i = 1, i \[LessEqual] 20, \(i++\), 
        AppendTo[dif, \((i + 1)\)\^2 - i\^2]];\)\), "\n", 
    \(dif\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
So, the differences between consecutive squares are the odd \
integers.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
But there is a much better way: start with a list of the numbers \
1,2,\[Ellipsis],50, square them all, and then subtract the appropriate \
portions of the list of squares.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Range[20]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(L = Range[20]\^2\)], "Input"],

Cell["Drop[L,1] - Drop[L,-1]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Combinations of list-based and functional programming are often \
particularly effective. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
NestList[ 1/(1 + #)&, 1, 15 ]
1 + % // N
GoldenRatio // N\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Rule-based Programming", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Another key idea in Mathematica is the application of substitution \
rules. A rule describes a certain pattern to look for, and a replacement if \
the pattern is found. If you come from a classical programming background, \
this is terra incognita.  You will get used to it quickly, though.\
\>", \
"Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
For example, the next rule merges two consecutive occurrences of an \
element in a lists into one. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(killdouble = {xx___, a_, a_, yy___} \[Rule] {xx, a, yy}\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
The triple underscores indicate any sequence of objects (including \
the empty sequence), and the single underscore indicates one single object. \
\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Here is one application of the rule to a random binary \
sequence:\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(bits = Table[Random[Integer], {20}]\), "\n", 
    \(bits /. \[InvisibleSpace]killdouble\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["And a few more", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
% /. killdouble
% /. killdouble
% /. killdouble\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Apply the rule till nothing changes anymore (note the double slash, the \
full command name is  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(ReplaceRepeated\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ")."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["bits //. killdouble", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Note that the rule cannot be applied any longer: there are no more \
double occurrences.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
The next rule is conditional: it applies only subject to a certain \
order condition.  Surprisingly, repeated application of the rule brings the \
elements of a list into sorted order. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(rule = {xx___, a_, b_, yy___} \[RuleDelayed] {xx, b, a, yy} /; 
          b < a\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(nums = Table[Random[Integer, 20], {20}]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["nums //. rule", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Rule based programming is very powerful, but there is a price you \
have to pay: if the pattern is complicated, it may be painfully slow to make \
the replacements.\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Two Examples", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Example 1:  The Factorial Function", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
The factorial function is defined on non-negative integers as \
follows:\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`fac(0)\  = \ 1\)]],
  "\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`fac(n)\  = \ n\ \[CenterDot]\ \(fac(n - 1)\)\)]],
  "    for   ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`n\  > \ 0\)]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Since the factorial function is rather important, it is built in.\
\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Clear[f1,f2,f3,f4,f5,f6,f7,f8,f9]", "Input"],

Cell["f1[n_] := Factorial[n]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
f1[100]
f1 /@ Range[10]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
There are other built-ins that make it very easy to obtain the \
factorial function. The next solution below is based on calculus, the others \
simply use multiplication. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(f2[n_] := Gamma[n + 1]\), "\n", 
    \(f3[n_] := Times @@ Range[n]\), "\n", 
    \(f4[n_] := \[Product]\+\(i = 1\)\%n i\), "\n", 
    \(f5[n_] := Fold[Times, 1, Range[n]]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can use any of the standard procedural looping constructs to define a \
factorial.  Below we use the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Module\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " construct\[LongDash]Mathematica's way of coping with local variables.  \
Note that this is essentially the same definition as one would write in a \
language such as Pascal or C/C++. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f6[n_] := \[IndentingNewLine]Module[{i, res}, \[IndentingNewLine]res = 
          1; \[IndentingNewLine]For[i = 1, i \[LessEqual] n, \(i++\), 
          res *= i]; \[IndentingNewLine]res\[IndentingNewLine]]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
f7[n_] :=
Module[{i,res},
\tres = 1;
\tDo[ res *= i, {i,n} ];
\tres
]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Recursion is another obvious line of attack.", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "First, we will describe an obvious way to define a recursive factorial \
function, then a slightly more elegant one. Note that in the definition of ",
  StyleBox["f9",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " below we must rely on ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to apply the rules in the proper order: first check whether the argument \
is 0, if not apply the second rule (which triggers a recursive call). "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["f8[n_] := If[ n==0, 1, n f7[n-1] ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
f9[0] = 1;
f9[n_] := n f9[n-1];\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Lastly, we can use substitution rules. ", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[frules, fac]\), "\n", 
    \(\(frules = {fac[0] \[Rule] 1, 
          fac[n_] \[RuleDelayed] n\ fac[n - 1]};\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(f10[n_] := fac[n] //. \[InvisibleSpace]frules;\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["All these functions produce the correct output values.", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Through[ {f1,f2,f3,f4,f5,f6,f7,f8,f9,f10}[10] ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
But, they are not equally efficient. Here are some timing results. \
\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We have to increase the system variable ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \($RecursionLimit\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to be able to use the recursive implementations on large inputs. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["$RecursionLimit = 10000;", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(test[ff_] := First[Timing[ff[2000]]]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["test /@ {f1,f2,f3,f4,f5,f6,f7,f8,f9,f10} // TableForm", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
As one might have expected, the first two methods are the best, and \
the last six are the worst. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that one has to be careful with timing results in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", repeated calculations may be hashed (without any request by the user), \
so that the running times improve as we repeat computations for the same \
inputs. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[" Example 2:  Cartesian Products", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Here are 3 solutions to the problem of computing the Cartesian \
product of two lists. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
The Cartesian product of two lists is defined to be the list of all \
pairs of elements where the first element in the pair is from the first list, \
and the second element is from the second list. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For example, on inputs ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \({a, b, c}\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \({0, 1}\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " we expect the output ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \({{a, 0}, {a, 1}, {b, 0}, {b, 1}, {c, 0}, {c, 1}}\)]],
  ", but not necessarily in this order."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The first solution that we will present is a brute force procedural \
attack.  The second solution uses the built-in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Table\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " construct for better efficiency.  Lastly, the fastest method is based on \
a somewhat obscure built-in: ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Distribute\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Be sure to look up ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Distribute\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " in the help browser."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(Cart0[a_List, b_List] := 
        Module[{i, j, res}, res = {}; 
          For[i = 1, i \[LessEqual] Length[a], \(i++\), 
            For[j = 1, 
              j \[LessEqual] 
                Length[b], \(j++\), \(res = 
                  Append[res, {a\[LeftDoubleBracket]i\[RightDoubleBracket], 
                      b\[LeftDoubleBracket]j\[RightDoubleBracket]}];\)]]; 
          res];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Cart1[a_List, b_List] := 
        Flatten[Table[{a\[LeftDoubleBracket]i\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              b\[LeftDoubleBracket]j\[RightDoubleBracket]}, {i, 
              Length[a]}, {j, Length[b]}], 1];\)\), "\n", 
    \(\(Cart2[a_List, b_List] := Distribute[{a, b}, List];\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The timing results show that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Distribute\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the right way to go. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
A = Range[40];
B = Range[40];
Cart0[ A, B ]; // Timing
Cart1[ A, B ]; // Timing
Cart2[ A, B ]; // Timing\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Brief Comment: External Programs", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As mentioned earlier,  ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " really consists of a compute engine, the kernel, and a display engine, \
the frontend.  It is possible to communicate with the kernel directly, from \
any other program running on the same machine (and even over the network).  \
The communications facility is called ",
  StyleBox["MathLink", "SmallText",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Actually, the frontend and the kernel use ",
  StyleBox["MathLink", "SmallText",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to exchange data."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["lnk = Links[]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["LinkError[ lnk[[1]] ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For advanced applications, it is possible to use this facility to speed up \
some crucial calculations considerably by implementing them in C++ code.  \
This code can then be called up in the same way that the built-in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are invoked.  For the time being, however, we ignore ",
  StyleBox["MathLink", "SmallText",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
  "Understanding ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Calculations"
}], "Section",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Most calculations in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can be executed in black-box mode, ",
  StyleBox["i.e.",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " without any understanding of what goes on under the hood.  On occasion, \
however, a bit more knowledge is required.  ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a rather large system, and to understand it fully one has to spend \
quite a bit of time.  Wolfram's manual is a good source, as is the help \
browser.  There are also some 100 books available on ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that provide more information on its structure and use.  However, the \
basic ideas are not all that complicated, and one can get good use out of the \
system knowing just a few fundamental facts. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Expressions", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Every object in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["expression",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Formally, an expression either is ",
  StyleBox["atomic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " or it is ",
  StyleBox["compound",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and has the form  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(h[e\_1, e\_2,  ... , e\_n]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   This is exactly the functional notation of mathematics."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In the above,  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ", "SmallText",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the ",
  StyleBox["head",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the expression, and the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(e\_i\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are other expressions, the ",
  StyleBox["subexpressions",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " or elements of the given expression. The number ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(n\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is the length of the expression.  Note that the head may itself well be a \
compound expression, so a term like ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(h[a]\)[b]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is perfectly acceptable and immensely useful. \nThis syntax is obvious in \
some cases"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["gg[a,b]", "Input"],

Cell["but also applies in cases that appear to be different:", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
{a,b,c}
% // FullForm\
\>", "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Atoms", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Atoms",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are the basic, indecomposable data objects in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  There are essentially three types of atoms in Mathematica: numbers, \
symbols, and strings. The ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Head\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " function a priori makes sense only for compound expressions, but it also \
returns a value for atoms indication a useful ",
  StyleBox["type",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the value of an expression. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(AtomQ /@ {12345, 123\/456, 123.456, 
        a123, "\<I am a string\>", {1, 2, 3}}\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
12345 // Head
123/456 // Head 
1+2/3 //Head
123.456 // Head 
a123 // Head
\"I am a string\" // Head
{1,2,3} // Head
x+y // Head\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Unlike with compound expressions you cannot use the head to build \
up the atom. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Integer[5] // FullForm
5 // FullForm
5 // Head\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?Integer\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["But note that this works:", "Text"],

Cell["Symbol[\"a5\"] // FullForm", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
However, for rationals and complex numbers we find these results:\
\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Rational[1,2]
Complex[1,2]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(1 + 2\ \[ImaginaryI] // FullForm\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(x + y\ \[ImaginaryI] // FullForm\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, one has to take the notion that atoms are indecomposable with a grain \
of salt.  These are some of the examples of non-uniformity in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica.  ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Another example concerns subscripts."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(x\_i\  // \ FullForm\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(x\_i\  // \ Head\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Compound Expressions", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Heads and Parts", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is a generic example of a ",
  StyleBox["compound expression",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". Its head is a symbol, and there are three subexpressions at the top \
level. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[exp, a, ff, gg, b, c, d]\), "\n", 
    \(t = exp[a, ff[b, b, gg[c]], d]\), "\n", 
    \(Head[t]\), "\n", 
    \(Length[t]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "One can access all the subexpressions using the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Part\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " command. Note that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Part\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " also accepts negative indices, a very useful feature in practice. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(t\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]\), "\n", 
    \(t\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]\), "\n", 
    \(t\[LeftDoubleBracket]\(-1\)\[RightDoubleBracket]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
One can access subexpressions at lower levels by using a sequence \
of indices.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(t\[LeftDoubleBracket]2, \(-1\), 1\[RightDoubleBracket]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Alternatively, there is a shorthand notation using double brackets \
that is inspired by array access in other programming languages. \
\>", "Text",\

  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(t\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket]\), "\n", 
    \(t\[LeftDoubleBracket]2, \(-1\), 1\[RightDoubleBracket]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The nesting depth of an expression is computed by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Depth\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Level\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " command allows one to extract subexpressions at various levels.  Note \
that the depth of an atom is 1, not 0 as one might expect. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Depth[a]\), "\n", 
    \(Depth[t]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(t\), "\n", 
    \(Level[t, 2]\), "\n", 
    \(Level[t, {2}]\), "\n", 
    \(Level[t, {\(-1\)}]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Display and FullForm", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Sometimes, expressions are displayed in a special way to make them \
easier to read for humans and conforms to standard notation in mathematics \
and computer science (more or less, at least). \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Plus[a, b, c]\), "\n", 
    \(a + b + c\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(List[a, b, c]\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \({a, b, c}\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
But internally the expressions are not at all affected by \
this:\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["% // FullForm", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Of course, we can also type in expressions in standard form.  Sometimes, \
the apparently clumsy internal form is slightly more expressive than the \
standard notation.  For example, the following cannot be reproduced by typing \
some expression involving ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["+",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["*",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Plus[]
Times[]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "FullForm is often useful to understand the structure of an expression \
better.  This can be very important for pattern matching, as the user may \
search for the wrong pattern if the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(FullForm\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is not understood."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(x\_5\/2\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(x\_5\/2 // FullForm\)}], "Input"],

Cell["x5/2  /. Rational[_,_] -> 111", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
x_List // FullForm
(x_List -> 5 x) // FullForm
First[ (x_List -> 5 x) ]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Manipulating Expressions", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Mathematica contains numerous commands that manipulate expressions \
in general, though at first glance one might suspect them to work on lists \
only. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["t = exp[a, ff[ b, b, gg[c] ], d];", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
First[ t ]
Rest[ t ]
RotateLeft[ t ]
Insert[ t, aaa, 2 ]
Drop[ t, 1 ]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Replacement of specific subexpressions is handled by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(ReplacePart\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["ReplacePart[ t, h[d], 2 ]", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
And, of course, one can perform substitutions everywhere in an \
expression:\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
t /. {b -> 123}
t /. {b -> t}\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Mathematica supports a feeble type structure. For example, one can define \
functions to operate only on inputs of type ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Integer\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", but by and large ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is really untyped. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Clear[f,a]
f[xx_Integer] := x^3;
f[5]
f[a]\
\>", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Rewrite Rules and Simplification", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For many specific headers there are rules that allow Mathematica to \
transform and simplify expressions.  For example, if the header is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Plus\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and all the subexpressions are integers, one can simply calculate their \
sum and return the corresponding integer (an atomic expression). "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Plus[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\), "\n", 
    \(AtomQ[Plus[1, 1]]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Plus[ Times[a,5], Times[a,10], Times[10,20] ] ", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "One can obtain a trace of the simplification procedure using ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Trace\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Plus[ Times[a,5], Times[a,10], Times[10,20] ]  // Trace", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "One can see here, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Times[a, 5]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is first transformed into ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Times[5, a]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Times[10, 20]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is directly evaluated, and so on, until the final result appears. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
For other headers, such as Integrate, there is an immensely \
complicated algorithm that performs the rewrite operation. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
As always, the user can specify certain rules for user-defined \
symbols.  For example, we can make a function distribute over products. \
\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[h, a, b, c]\), "\n", 
    \(h /: h[x_\ y_] := h[x] + h[y]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h[a\ b\ c]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["The logarithm function has precisely this property.", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Of course, rewrite rules can also be defined explicitly by the \
user. For example, we can replace all occurrences of an expression by \
another. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[x, z]\), "\n", 
    \(Sin[x] + x\^2 /. \[InvisibleSpace]x \[Rule] z\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Replacements can be made repeatedly until no more changes occur, \
i.e., until a fixed point has been reached with respect to the given rules. \
\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[f, a]\), "\n", 
    \(t = Nest[f, a, 5]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(t //. \[InvisibleSpace]f[x_] \[Rule] x\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
One can apply multiple rules simultaneously, but the results can \
sometimes be strange.\
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Sin[x] + x\^2 /. \[InvisibleSpace]{x \[Rule] z, 
        x\^2 \[Rule] x\^2}\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Main Evaluation Loop", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Basic Structure of Evaluation", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "When the user selects an input cell and presses the ",
  StyleBox["Enter",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " key, the corresponding expression is sent to the kernel.  The kernel then \
tries to evaluate all the subexpressions, inserts the results into the \
expression, and then performs all the simplification and transformation rules \
it knows about (user-defined as well as built-in). Whenever no more rules \
apply and the current expression can not be transformed any further, the \
kernel returns that expression as the result of the calculation."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, here is a very rough outline of the main evaluation function in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Eval[  exp ]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  := \n\n",
  StyleBox["if",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "    (  exp is atom  )\n\n",
  StyleBox["then",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "   return value of atom\n\n",
  StyleBox["else   \n    ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "let   exp ==  h[e1,e2,...,en] \n    compute  v  = Eval[h]\n    compute  vi \
= Eval[ei]  for all  i = 1,...,n\n    res = FixedPoint[  \n    \tapply to \
v[v1,v2,...,vn]  all known\n\t\trewrite/simplification rules ]\n\t\t\t\n",
  StyleBox["return",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " res"
}], "Program",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Thus,  Eval  is recursive, and uses a fixed point mechanism. And, \
reality is quite a bit more complicated than what we have just described. For \
example, there are ways to suppress evaluation of subexpressions, a feature \
that is crucial in many functions. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Evaluation in Practice", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The main evaluation loop behaves fairly naturally most of the time, and \
does what one would expect from the system in the first place. Here is an \
example.\nFirst, assign ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(x + a\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(y\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[a, x, y]\), "\n", 
    \(y = x + a\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If we change the value of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(x\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", say, to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(z\^2\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(y\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " remains unaffected, but if we ask for the value of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(y\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " the evaluation loop kicks in, and returns ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(a + z\^2\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["x = z^2", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["?y", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["y", "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can see the activity using ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Trace\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Trace[y]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["A longer slightly chain of evaluation steps. ", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(x = \(-a\); \), "\n", 
    \(Trace[y]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Sometimes, one would much like to suspend evaluation, for example, to do \
pattern matching, or in order to be able to obtain a ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(FullForm\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " of an expression. This can be done with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Hold\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(HoldForm\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(FullForm[2 + 3]\), "\n", 
    \(FullForm[Hold[2 + 3]]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[F]\), "\n", 
    \(F[0] = 0; \), "\n", 
    \(F[1] = 1; \), "\n", 
    \(F[n_] := F[n - 1] + F[n - 2]; \)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(trc = Flatten[Trace[F[6], F[_Integer]]]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Count[trc, F[1]]\), "\n", 
    \(Count[trc, HoldForm[F[1]]]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For simple expression, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Trace\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " displays the inner workings of the main loop fairly well. Be warned, \
though, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Trace\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " tends to produce huge amounts of output and has to be used with caution.  \
See the help browser for ways to fine tune ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Trace\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Another crucial issue is suspended evaluation. For example, to do pattern \
matching, or in order to be able to obtain a ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(FullForm\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " of an expression, one needs to be able to stop evaluation. This can be \
done with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(Hold\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(HoldForm\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(FullForm[2 + 3]\), "\n", 
    \(FullForm[Hold[2 + 3]]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[F]\), "\n", 
    \(F[0] = 0; \), "\n", 
    \(F[1] = 1; \), "\n", 
    \(F[n_] := F[n - 1] + F[n - 2]; \)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(trc = Flatten[Trace[F[6], F[_Integer]]]\)], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Count[trc, F[1]]\), "\n", 
    \(Count[trc, HoldForm[F[1]]]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["Another example. ", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[f, ff]\), "\n", 
    \(f[x_Integer] := x\^2\), "\n", 
    \(\(ff = f;\)\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(FullForm[Hold[ff @@ gg[5 + 5]]]\), "\n", 
    \(Trace[ff @@ gg[5 + 5]]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
And another one, this time an example of how a head may be \
evaluated.  \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Clear[f, g, h]\), "\n", 
    \(f[0] = g; \), "\n", 
    \(f[1] = h; \), "\n", 
    \(g[x_] := x\^2; \), "\n", 
    \(h[x_] := x + 5; \), "\n", 
    \(Trace[\(f[Random[Integer]]\)[3]]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->True]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Interrupting Evaluation", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell["\<\
Because of recursion, and the fixed point mechanism, things may go \
wrong in several ways.  For example, the user may have defined rules that \
lead into a cycle:  some expression evaluates to another, which then in turn \
evaluates to the first. Also, the evaluation may fail because of lacking \
resources (time and/or space), despite the fact that the expression in \
question is sound in principle. \
\>", "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "In an emergency, you can interrupt or terminate a computation by pulling \
down the ",
  StyleBox["Kernel",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " menu, and selecting the items ",
  StyleBox["Interrupt Evaluation",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["Abort Evaluation",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ".  If that does not work, you may have to ",
  StyleBox["quit the kernel",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "\[LongDash]which means you lose all your definitions but the notebook \
remains intact.  If you simply wait till the whole session crashes, the \
notebook may also be destroyed.  In some cases, though, it is possible to \
save a notebook during an evaluation.  Note that interrupts and aborts do not \
necessarily happen instantaneously, it make take quite a while till the \
computation reaches a place where it can be conveniently stopped. "
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

